<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Horatio Nelson</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1758">1758</a>–<a href="/years/1805">1805</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Horatio Nelson, 1st Viscount Nelson, Lord Nelson</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Horatio Nelson was a British admiral, the most celebrated naval commander of the Revolutionary and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a>. While serving in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-indies">West Indies</a> in the 1780s, he married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frances-nelson-viscountess-nelson">Frances Nisbet</a> on Nevis in 1787. He won the battles of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> in 1798 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a> in 1801, and he was killed at the moment of his greatest victory, over the French and Spanish fleets at Trafalgar on 21 October 1805.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/horatio-nelson.jpg" alt="Horatio Nelson" width="320" height="386" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lemuel Francis Abbott · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HoratioNelson1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Denmark joined the League of Armed Neutrality, a British fleet under Hyde Parker and <b>Horatio Nelson</b> defeated the Danish fleet at the Battle of Copenhagen in 1801.</blockquote><a href="/empire/denmark-norway">Denmark-Norway · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The American War of Independence ended in defeat after the surrender at Yorktown in 1781, but from 1793 Britain led coalitions against revolutionary France, and <b>Horatio Nelson</b> destroyed a French fleet at the Battle of the Nile in 1798.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1798 Ferdinand attacked the French in Rome, but his army was routed; the royal family fled to Palermo on <b>Nelson</b>'s ships, and in January 1799 French troops and Neapolitan patriots proclaimed the Parthenopean Republic.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-naples">Kingdom of Naples · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Horatio Nelson</b>’s victory at the Battle of Trafalgar in 1805 ended the threat of French invasion, and the Duke of Wellington’s campaigns in the Peninsular War and his victory, with Prussian forces, at the Battle of Waterloo in 1815 helped bring down Napoleon.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-great-britain-and-ireland">United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Because Denmark had joined the League of Armed Neutrality, a British fleet under Sir Hyde Parker, with <b>Horatio Nelson</b> leading the attack, engaged the Danish-Norwegian fleet before Copenhagen.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-2">2 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After a vast procession through London, <b>Horatio Nelson</b> was laid to rest in the crypt of St Paul's.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-9">9 January in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Burnham Thorpe, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Cape Trafalgar, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.94,0.76&amp;year=1788" aria-label="Burnham Thorpe on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4567 340 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Horatio Nelson"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5017 610h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5017 610h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.94,0.76&amp;year=1788">What was here in 1788? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I have only one eye,—I have a right to be blind sometimes…&#8221;</p><footer>At the Battle of Copenhagen, 1801, on the signal to break off &#183; original &#183; as reported in Robert Southey's Life of Nelson (1813) &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/947/pg947-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…a great and glorious victory, and may no misconduct in any one tarnish it; and may humanity after victory be the predominant feature in the British fleet!&#8221;</p><footer>Prayer written before Trafalgar, 1805 &#183; original &#183; from his diary, as given in Robert Southey's Life of Nelson (1813) &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/947/pg947-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Before this time to-morrow I shall have gained a peerage or Westminster Abbey.&#8221;</p><footer>To his officers before the Battle of the Nile, 1798 &#183; original &#183; as reported in Robert Southey's Life of Nelson (1813) &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/947/pg947-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>2 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;It is warm work, and this day may be the last to any of us at a moment…&#8221;</p><footer>At the Battle of Copenhagen, 1801 &#183; original &#183; Southey's account &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/947/pg947-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;If this is the reward for a faithful discharge of my duty, I shall be careful, and never stand forward again. But I have done my duty, and have nothing to accuse myself of.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter from the West Indies (1785), when others were thanked for his seizures &#183; original &#183; as quoted in Southey's Life of Nelson &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/947/pg947-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
